    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fountain Hill takes its name from a prominent hill that sits between two natural springs, which once powered early farms and mills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fountain_hill_arkansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the 1818 Quapaw treaty opened the area, settlers from the South arrived in the 1840s, establishing farms on a ridge between Flat Creek and Fountain Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fountain_hill_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Local drainage follows Bayou Bartholomew, which flows southward into the Ouachita River basin, shaping the town’s water system.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fountain_hill_arkansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fountain Hill’s surrounding pine forests have long supported timber production, with reforestation efforts restoring much of the canopy after historic logging.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fountain_hill_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The town experiences hot summers with July highs around 92°F, but its flat terrain also makes it vulnerable to occasional tornadoes.",
